WEBVTT

s01
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Binary search needs sorted data. When the middle value is too small, everything to its left is too small as well. In an unsorted list, that shortcut does not work.

s02
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Keep the remaining indices in a closed interval, from low to high. Both ends are still candidates. For seven items, start with low at zero and high at six.

s03
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Choose the middle index by adding half the distance to low and rounding the distance down. This also avoids adding two potentially large indices together.

s04
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:04.000
After a comparison, return the middle index if it matches. If the value is too small, move low to mid plus one. If it is too large, move high to mid minus one.

s05
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The middle value is twenty-one, which is smaller than thirty-four. Everything on the left, including twenty-one, can be ruled out.

s06
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The next middle value is forty-two. It is too large, so rule it out and discard everything to its right.

s07
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Only index four remains. Its value is thirty-four, so we return four. That took three comparisons.

s08
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Each miss cuts the remaining range roughly in half. That is logarithmic growth. Even a million sorted items take at most twenty comparisons in this loop.

s09
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Equal boundaries mean one candidate is left, so compare it. Stop with not found only when low is greater than high. Then the closed interval is empty.

s10
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Do not leave mid inside the interval after a miss. With two candidates, the rounded middle can equal low. Setting low to mid would repeat the same comparison forever.
